Q: HTML text-overflow ellipsis detection A: <pre><code>function isTextEllipsed(el: HTMLElement) {
    // 1. create a range and select the text content of the element
    const range = document.createRange();
    range.selectNodeContents(el);

    // 2. get the bounding rect of the text itself (fractional)
    const textWidth = range.getBoundingClientRect().width;

    // 3. get the bounding rect of the container (fractional)
    // subtracting padding to get the &quot;available&quot; space
    const style = window.getComputedStyle(el);
    const paddingLeft = parseFloat(style.paddingLeft);
    const paddingRight = parseFloat(style.paddingRight);
    const availableWidth = el.getBoundingClientRect().width - paddingLeft - paddingRight;

    // 4. compare text width to available space
    // using small 0.5px buffer to account for browser anti-aliasing quirks
    return textWidth &gt; availableWidth + 0.5;
  }
</code></pre>

Q: fetch retry request (on failure) A: <p>The following code makes 10 attempts to send a request in case of 'Failed to fetch' error, in case of other errors it is issued immediately</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>async function persistentFetch(url, args) 
{
    let attempts = 10;
    while (attempts-- &gt; 0) {
        try {
            return await fetch(url, args)
                .then(async function (res) {
                    if (res.ok) {
                        return res.json();
                    } else { // server error
                        let errtext = await res.text();
                        const error = new Error(errtext);
                        error._isServerError = true;
                        throw error;
                    }
                });
        } catch (err) {
            if (err._isServerError)
                throw err;
        }
        await new Promise(resolve =&gt; setTimeout(resolve, 1000)); // delay
    }
    switch (navigator.language.slice(0, 2).toLowerCase()) {
        case 'ru': throw new Error('Нестабильное интернет-соединение, повторите попытку.');
        default: throw new Error('Unstable internet connection, try again.');
    }
}
</code></pre>

Q: Map List&lt;&gt; with Automapper? A: <p>Yes, you need to tell AutoMapper about each mapping. It will not guess for you. So, if an <code>OrderDTO</code> should map to an <code>Order</code>, you must tell AutoMapper that. You must also specify the reverse relationship if that's needed as well (i.e. <code>Order</code> should map to <code>OrderDTO</code>).</p>

<p>In other words, for bi-directional mapping you would need:</p>

<pre><code>Mapper.CreateMap&lt;Order, OrderDTO&gt;();
Mapper.CreateMap&lt;OrderDTO, Order&gt;();
</code></pre>

<p>As far as <code>Customer</code> goes, if both <code>Customer</code> and <code>CustomerDTO</code> have a property named <code>Orders</code>, you don't need to do anything else. As long as you've told AutoMapper to map between <code>Order</code> and <code>OrderDTO</code> and <code>Customer</code> and <code>CustomerDTO</code>, it will automatically map your <code>Order</code> when you map <code>Customer</code>.</p>

Q: Parsing XML with PHP. Duplicate node names A: <p>You can use xPath:</p>

<pre><code> &lt;?php
$sxml = simplexml_load_string('&lt;company&gt;
&lt;name&gt;Test Co&lt;/name&gt;
&lt;link href="link1" rel="self"/&gt;
&lt;link href="link2" rel="www"/&gt;
&lt;/company&gt;');


$link = $sxml-&gt;xPath('//link[@rel="www"]');

var_dump($link);
</code></pre>

